g at a rate of $5in/min$. The diameter of the cone is always $3$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6in$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186064623 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $3$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3h \\\\ 2r & =3h \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi (6)^{2}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064767 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064767 1071 "$10\\cdot (-0.7)=$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064767 1071 "What is $10 \\cdot (-0.7)$?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186064767 1074 "We have a product of a positive number $(10)$ and a negative number $(-0.7).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n10\\cdot (-0.7)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $10$ by $0.7$ without any signs: $10\\cdot 0.7=7.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n10\\cdot (-0.7)=-7.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064905 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064905 1071 "![](images/aa/aa0da0d113d1077b1a394f2f45fe839f8845d1bbe544e2497582df18e079c52a.png)\n\nA drone $D$ is approaching a landing point $L$ on the ground at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to the horizontal. The drone's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the drone. If the drone is approaching the ground with a speed of $6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ what is the speed of the drone's shadow?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064905 1071 "![](images/aa/aa0da0d113d1077b1a394f2f45fe839f8845d1bbe544e2497582df18e079c52a.png)\n\nA drone $D$ is approaching a landing point $L$ on the ground at an angle of $\\frac{π}{4}$ radians to the horizontal. The drone's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the drone. If the drone is approaching the ground with a speed of $6ft/s$, what is the speed of the drone's shadow?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186064905 1074 "![](images/9c/9cb94ea5cab43d48da6b836026d302504d7e63fe1260aa81a2b577436b6b4e98.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between the landing point and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the drone, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ (negative because the distance between the landing point and the drone is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-6)=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064964 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064964 1071 "The radius of the base of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h}.$ The height of the cone is always half its radius. At what rate is the cone's volume decreasing when the radius of its base is $12\\,\\text{m}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186064964 1071 "The radius of the base of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $4m/h$. The height of the cone is always half its radius. At what rate is the cone's volume decreasing when the radius of its base is $12m$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186064964 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{r}{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065179 1061 28082 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065179 1061 631148 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065180 1061 28084 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065180 1061 631141 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065330 1061 943 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065330 1061 631140 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065331 1061 944 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065331 1061 631145 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065332 1061 948 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065332 1061 631153 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065387 1064 "Suppose that a jet takes off and climbs with an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nradians to the horizontal. The speed of the jet is $462\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}$ and the angle of elevation does not change. How fast is the altitude of the jet increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a diagram depicting the given information.\n\n![](images/70/708ba0a80dd7c8e3e58e1456ad643338a8e630ec45aedb8955b042ddac92c329.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the jet's height from the ground (i.e., its altitude), $z$ is the jet's distance from its take-off point. The quantity that we're required to calculate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z$. Relative to the angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nthe sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (\\frac{π}{6}) &= \\frac{y}{z} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{y}{z} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dz}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 462\\,\\textrm{km/h}\n$$\n\nso plugging this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 462 = 231\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $231\\,\\textrm{km/h}$." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065387 1064 "Suppose that a jet takes off and climbs with an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to the horizontal. The speed of the jet is $462\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}$ and the angle of elevation does not change. How fast is the altitude of the jet increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a diagram depicting the given information.\n\n![](images/70/708ba0a80dd7c8e3e58e1456ad643338a8e630ec45aedb8955b042ddac92c329.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the jet's height from the ground (i.e., its altitude), $z$ is the jet's distance from its take-off point. The quantity that we're required to calculate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 462\\,\\textrm{km/h},$ so plugging this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 462 = 231.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $231\\,\\textrm{km/h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065388 1064 "Consider a $2\\,\\text{m}$ ladder that leans against a wall, forming an angle $\\theta$ with the ground. The bottom of the ladder is pushed toward the wall at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nas shown below. How fast is the angle $\\theta$ increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a figure according to the given information.\n\n![](images/16/16996176a8557dc71fe3f3a3e0d2a0c91e5b164b9eeeeeb6cf76b44952642a47.png)\n\nIn the figure, $z$ is the length of the ladder and $x$ the distance of the bottom of the ladder from the wall. The quantity $z=2$ does not change with time because the length of the ladder is fixed.\n\nOur job is to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z$. Relative to the angle $\\theta$, the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{x}{z} \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos θ) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\\n- \\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the bottom of the ladder is pushed against the wall at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n- \\sin θ \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= -\\frac{1}{4\\sin θ} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\csc θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065388 1064 "Consider a $2\\,\\text{m}$ ladder that leans against a wall, forming an angle $\\theta$ with the ground. The bottom of the ladder is pushed toward the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ as shown below. How fast is the angle $\\theta$ increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a figure according to the given information.\n\n![](images/16/16996176a8557dc71fe3f3a3e0d2a0c91e5b164b9eeeeeb6cf76b44952642a47.png)\n\nIn the figure,  $z$ is the length of the ladder and $x$ the distance of the bottom of the ladder from the wall. The quantity $z=2$ does not change with time because the length of the ladder is fixed.\n\nOur job is to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the bottom of the ladder is pushed against the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186065389 1064 "We can use differentiation to solve related rates problems in the context of cones. In general, we follow the usual three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n**Step 2:** Write down a relation between the time-dependent variables.\n\n**Step 3:** Differentiate the relation with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nBefore we start, recall that the volume $v$ of a right cone of radius $r$ and height $h$ (shown below) is given by the formula\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\n![](images/cf/cf4e3b5b9f0d827bccaee008e006ad62d870fb2d782df8dd94f61bc4a3ca4050.png)" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186065389 1064 "We can use differentiation to solve related rates problems in the context of cones. In general, we follow the usual three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n**Step 2:** Write down a relation between the time-dependent variables.\n\n**Step 3:** Differentiate the relation with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nBefore we start, recall that the volume $v$ of a right cone of radius $r$ and height $h$ (shown below) is given by the formula\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\n![](images/cf/cf4e3b5b9f0d827bccaee008e006ad62d870fb2d782df8dd94f61bc4a3ca4050.png)" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186066976 1078 17592186066981 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186068720 1038 17592186523930 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186068720 1038 17592186523953 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186068721 1038 17592186523992 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186068894 1038 17592186524019 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186068925 1038 17592186523950 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186068926 1038 17592186523928 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186069301 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186069301 1071 "![](images/cd/cd878559d6be37faa60bdb33cbebe2f124d09744aa4df7dae40372499116f239.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $CD^(―)$?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186069301 1071 "![](images/cd/cd878559d6be37faa60bdb33cbebe2f124d09744aa4df7dae40372499116f239.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{CD}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186069301 1074 "From the figure, we note that the points $C$ and $D$ have coordinates ${{(-5,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(2,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{CD},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-5))^{2}+(-1-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186072800 1078 17592186072804 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186073739 1038 17592186524017 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186074427 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186074427 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\n> Mark and James shared slices of pizza in the ratio of $2: 6$, so there are $0$ slices of Mark's share for each slice of James' share." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186074427 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nMark and James shared slices of pizza in the ratio of $2:6$, so there are $\\boxed{}$ slices of Mark's share for each slice of James' share." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186074427 1074 "We can rewrite the ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{2÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThat means that there are $\\frac{1}{3}$ slices of Mark's share for each slice of James' share." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087544 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087544 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186087546 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087546 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0{)}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186087710 1038 17592186523946 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087842 1038 17592186523962 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087843 1038 17592186523945 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087843 1038 17592186523997 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087844 1038 17592186523981 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186087895 1050 1500.0 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186088921 1064 "A **vertical asymptote** is a vertical line to which a function gets very close but never touches.\n\nFor example, let's consider the rational function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79e02370d60aec1e1e7f9e21dafbb876ea1f9b9b2de2b3d2b8b6ba14ffabfe3d.png)\n\nThe two dotted lines $x=2$ and $x=-2$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. The function gets closer and closer to these lines but never touches them.\n\nBut how can we calculate the vertical asymptotes of a function without using a graph?\n\nIn general, we can find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function using the following procedure:\n\n- Factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors.\n\n- Set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nIn our case, the function is already factored, and there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, to find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+2) = 0\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are $x=2$ and $x=-2.$  Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ This matches up with what we saw in the graph." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186088921 1064 "A **vertical asymptote** is a vertical line to which a function gets very close but never touches.\n\nFor example, let's consider the rational function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79e02370d60aec1e1e7f9e21dafbb876ea1f9b9b2de2b3d2b8b6ba14ffabfe3d.png)\n\nThe two dotted lines $x=2$ and $x=-2$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. The function gets closer and closer to these lines but never touches them.\n\nBut how can we calculate the vertical asymptotes of a function without using a graph?\n\nIn general, we can find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function using the following procedure:\n\n1\\. Factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors.\n\n2\\. Set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nIn our case, the function is already factored, and there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, to find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+2) = 0\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are $x=2$ and $x=-2.$  Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ This matches up with what we saw in the graph." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186092240 1038 17592186523970 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186092382 1038 17592186523927 13194139535619 175921