istic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186101551 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186101551 1071 "An influenza epidemic is spreading through a community of $700000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with the flu is jointly proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t$, measured in days, and the number of uninfected people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.6$, which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)$?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186101551 1071 "An influenza epidemic is spreading through a community of $700\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with the flu is jointly proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ measured in days,  and the number of uninfected people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.6,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186101551 1074 "Since the growth rate of infected people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected people, the number of infected people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.6,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=700\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have the flu is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P).\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186121511 1078 17592186121516 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186124609 1038 17592186523996 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186124819 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186124819 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y$ on the parabola $y - x^{2} = 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x, y)$ exists on the parabola.*" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186124819 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186124819 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y-{x}^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(-1,1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186129161 1038 17592186523935 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186147544 1038 17592186523969 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186152124 1038 17592186523984 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186162360 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186162360 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186162360 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=5\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186162360 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=5\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186165752 1075 17592186165753 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186165752 1075 17592186165754 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186165752 1075 17592186165755 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186165752 1075 17592186165756 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186165752 1075 17592186165757 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186165752 1075 17592186523262 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186165752 1075 17592186523319 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186165752 1075 17592186523346 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186165752 1075 17592186523541 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186165752 1075 17592186523704 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186165752 1078 17592186523346 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186166762 1038 17592186523987 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186166762 1038 17592186524008 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186166763 1038 17592186523940 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186166764 1038 17592186523925 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186166942 1038 17592186523972 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186166942 1038 17592186524032 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186167242 1071 "![](images/79/79017dcca6805220acfca563829e1feb28e00520ca095cdb4f19f0f16223aff8.png)\n\nThe ph