pha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.084\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186172338 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186172338 1071 "A survey conducted by a sports magazine on a random sample of $125$ students from various U.S. universities reported that $40$ respondents love soccer. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of college students who love soccer.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186172338 1071 "A survey conducted by a sports magazine on a random sample of $125$ students from various U.S. universities reported that $40$ respondents love soccer. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of college students who love soccer. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186172338 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all college students in the US,\n\n- some of the students surveyed love soccer, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who love soccer can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{40}{125}=0.32\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=125\\cdot 0.32=40>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=125\\cdot (1-0.32)=85>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.32\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{125}} \\\\  & 0.32\\pm 0.107\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186173220 1064 "We know that if a random variable $X$ follows a *discrete* uniform distribution, then it has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/0a/0abfcf5eccaaaf22dbc144f58098d394297a5819a1857ff316ce9e9d01e44f7d.svg)\n\nLet's now suppose that \n\n$$\nX\\sim U\\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are integers. In other words, we're concerned with the special case where the support $S$ of $X$ contains every integer between $a$ and $b$ inclusive. \n\nIn this case, it can be shown that the mean value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186173220 1064 "We know that if a random variable $X$ follows a *discrete* uniform distribution, then it has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that\n\n$$\nX\\sim U\\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are integers. In other words, we're concerned with the special case where the support $S$ of $X$ contains every integer between $a$ and $b$ inclusive.\n\nIn this case, it can be shown that the mean value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\left.\\dfrac{\\mid S\\right|^2-1}{12}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186173221 1064 "Here, we will show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nBy the definition of expected value for discrete random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S)xf(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x ∈ S)x \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\begin{vmatrix}} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid}∑_(x ∈ S)x \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}}[a + (a + 1) + (a + 2) + ⋯ + b]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the term in the square brackets is an arithmetic series with the first term $a$ and the last term $b$. Therefore, using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\begin{vmatrix}}[a + (a + 1) + (a + 2) + ⋯ + b] \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &} \\cdot \\frac{\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}}{2} \\cdot (a + b) \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &} \\cdot \\frac{\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}}{2} \\cdot (a + b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{a + b}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186173221 1064 "Here, we will show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/36/36e73322640df22386428fdc41d1940ef7469587566c2f4f24936158292c087c.svg)\n\nBy the definition of expected value for discrete random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}xf(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}[a+(a+1)+(a+2)+⋯+b].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the term in the square brackets is an arithmetic series with the first term $a$ and the last term $b.$ Therefore, using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}[a+(a+1)+(a+2)+⋯+b] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{|S|}{2}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{|S|}}{2}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{a+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186173222 1064 "Let's now show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\left.\\dfrac{\\mid S\\right|^2-1}{12}\n$$\n\nWe will assume, without loss of generality, that the distribution of our random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, \\mid S \\mid \\}\n$$\n\nWe do not lose generality here because, in general, the variance (i.e., spread) of a random variable is unaffected by translations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X + c] = \\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nBy the definition of $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S)x^{2}f(x) \\\\\n&= ∑_(x ∈ S)x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\begin{vmatrix}} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid} \\cdot ∑_(x ∈ S)x^{2} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} + 2^{2} + ⋯ + & S & ^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n i^2 = \\dfrac16 n (n+1)(2n+1)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} + 2^{2} + ⋯ + & S & ^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot & S\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2 \\mid S \\mid + 1) \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\mid S \\mid} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot & S\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2 \\mid S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2 \\mid S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1))/(6)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2, \\qquad \\textrm {E}[X] = \\dfrac{1+ \\mid S \\mid}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - (E[X])^{2} \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(6) - (\\frac{\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(6) - ((\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1)^{2})/(4) \\\\\n&= (2(\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12) - (3(\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1)^{2})/(12) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12)(2(2\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1) - 3(\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}+ 1)) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12)(4\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 2 - 3\\end{vmatrix}S\\end{vmatrix}- 3) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1))/(12)(\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}- 1) \\\\\n&= ((\\begin{vmatrix}S & + 1)(\\mid S\\end{vmatrix}- 1))/(12) \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{\\mid S\\right|^{2} - 1}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186173222 1064 "Let's now show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume, without loss of generality, that the distribution of our random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, |S|\\}.\n$$\n\nWe do not lose generality here because, in general, the variance (i.e., spread) of a random variable is unaffected by translations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X + c] = \\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy the definition of $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}f(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot {\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n i^2 = \\dfrac16 n (n+1)(2n+1)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot |S|\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\cancel{|S|}\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2, \\qquad \\textrm {E}[X] = \\dfrac{1+|S|}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-{(\\text{E}[X])}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-{(\\frac{|S|+1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-\\frac{(|S|+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{12}-\\frac{3(|S|+1)^{2}}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(2(2|S|+1)-3(|S|+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(4|S|+2-3|S|-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(|S|-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(|S|-1)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186173914 1078 17592186173917 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174400 1078 17592186174404 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174496 1075 17592186174499 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186174496 1075 17592186174500 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186174496 1075 17592186174501 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186174496 1075 17592186523357 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174496 1075 17592186523492 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174496 1075 17592186523694 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174496 1078 17592186174498 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174502 1075 17592186174503 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186174502 1075 17592186174505 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186174502 1075 17592186174507 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186174502 1075 17592186523557 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174502 1075 17592186523596 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174502 1075 17592186523759 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186174502 1078 17592186174504 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175688 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{3}(x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $y\\gt 1$." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175688 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/ef/ef996369f785405f40f576aafc3b0ec3a5045a38ee36636908c3ea91f976d94f.svg) \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $y\\gt 1.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175688 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2,\\; y \\gt 1\n$$\n\nand a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b43fd1a1622f406b7679d9f0f34552e046b9bc94b75a3504677f30b347d283.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq x, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{y}\\int_{- ∞}^{x}f(u, v)dudv &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(u + v)dudv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} + uv]_{u = 0}^{u = x}dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + xv]dv \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{2}v}{2} + \\frac{xv^{2}}{2}]_{v = 0}^{v = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{x}{2} - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(x^{2} + x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175688 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0 \\leq x \\leq 2,\\;  y \\gt 1$ and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b43fd1a1622f406b7679d9f0f34552e046b9bc94b75a3504677f30b347d283.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq x, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(u+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xv]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2}+\\frac{x{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({x}^{2}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175859 1071 "![](images/46/46508c4856bb4a5086f7b21c3bf65758a0f9157179c661e63ae42944bd252cb9.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175859 1071 "![](images/46/46508c4856bb4a5086f7b21c3bf65758a0f9157179c661e63ae42944bd252cb9.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/9e/9eb73315957a9315c2ec10cbfa5e86c3f0bdd01617e8e68b3b1a8039b570b280.svg)\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175859 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\non our diagram. Let's also consider the region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/aa/aa4e587b8951f264d1f22392fd8fea7ae3397010376b82bbcd3ffab0d8dc50b8.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = P\\left(X< 2, Y< \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{5/2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{2} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 2 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{5/2}\\int_{- ∞}^{2}f(u, v)dudv &= \\int_{2}^{5/2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{u}vdudv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}u^{1/2}vdudv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}[\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}v]_{u = 0}^{u = 1}dv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}[\\frac{2}{5}u^{3/2}v]_{u = 0}^{u = 1}dv \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{5/2}\\frac{2}{5}vdv \\\\\n&= [\\frac{v^{2}}{5}]_{2}^{5/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4} - \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175859 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/aa/aa4e587b8951f264d1f22392fd8fea7ae3397010376b82bbcd3ffab0d8dc50b8.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = P\\left(X<                2, Y<                \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{5/2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{2} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 2 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{u}v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{1/2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{[\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}v]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{[\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{3/2}v]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}\\frac{2}{5}v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{5}]}_{2}^{5/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175860 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where\n$F(5,5) = 0.9, \\quad F(5,2) = 0.3, \\quad F(0,5) = 0.45, \\quad F(0,2) = 0.15$.\nFind $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 5, 2 \\leq Y \\leq 5)$." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175860 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,5) = 0.9, \\quad\nF(5,2) = 0.3, \\quad\nF(0,5) = 0.45, \\quad\nF(0,2) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nFind $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 5, 2 \\leq Y \\leq 5).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175860 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)\n$$\n\nis the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5)$.\n2. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}$.\n3. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}$. This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 \\le X \\le 5, 2 \\le Y \\le 5) &= P(X \\le 5, Y \\le 5)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]) \\\\\n&=-P(X \\le 5, Y \\le 2)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]) - P(X \\le 0, Y \\le 5)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]) \\\\\n&= + P(X \\le 0, Y \\le 2)^(⏞)^(top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]) \\\\\n&= F(5, 5) - F(5, 2) - F(0, 5) + F(0, 2) \\\\\n&= 0.9 - 0.3 - 0.45 + 0.15 \\\\\n&= 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175860 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175891 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 1,\\; y > 4$." 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175891 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/f7/f700fb9e7da84b3eb8d162847cada4ad69ae96b48da8b6e31f461272c19d0a17.svg)\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 1,\\; y > 4.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175891 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x > 1,\\; y >4$, and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8ad9c26a96468741d20e886a3c18c56e248e529e90fa5908b0b472efd01e75.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta = D\n$$\n\nSo our region $\\Delta$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{y}\\int_{- ∞}^{x}f(u, v)dudv &= ∬_{D}f(u, v)dudv = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 false] [17592186175891 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x > 1,\\; y >4,$ and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8ad9c26a96468741d20e886a3c18c56e248e529e90fa5908b0b472efd01e75.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta = D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $\\Delta$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175910 1038 17592186523988 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186175923 1038 17592186524010 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186176006 1038 17592186523934 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186176006 1038 17592186523951 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186176006 1038 17592186524007 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186176006 1038 17592186524016 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186176007 1038 17592186523944 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186176007 1038 17592186523947 131941