ue] [17592186523922 1088 17592186050975 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523922 1089 17592186523923 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523922 1090 #uuid "b28c771f-f4c4-59de-bbba-98b430a5c321" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524045 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524055 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524059 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524068 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524070 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524079 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524082 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524086 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1084 17592186524104 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1085 "![](images/d4/d4bff62d0370b252f4bbbbace8503b225d71b05388f67038dff60d10a33a00ac.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1086 #uuid "f93bccbd-7e87-5446-93d1-f17e4bdb0319" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523923 1087 17592186524079 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523924 1068 17592186523877 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523924 1069 "q-343195" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523924 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523924 1071 "![](images/22/2242cf493c6307d8c6f2dac778efe6f89ca4ba6f04203940076360613c8dc9b0.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ support pole leans against a vertical wall, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. The base of the pole $B$ is being pulled toward the wall at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ At what rate is $\\theta $ increasing at the moment when the top of the pole $T$ is $5\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523924 1072 #uuid "017bbabc-f897-558b-b361-4f91d645fa28" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523924 1074 "![](images/e0/e08cddcde0d709ebe455b8d8935174955798b877f054dab0de74d06035accc91.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the base of the pole to the wall, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the pole and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the pole. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the pole's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=5$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-2$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{13} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523925 1068 17592186523849 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523925 1069 "q-95894" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523925 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523925 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2x}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523925 1072 #uuid "04ba3fd8-1522-5fe9-a412-8ecb77ad06a4" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523925 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}^{′}\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot {(2x)}^{′}}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2{csch}^{2}⁡2x)\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot (2)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523926 1068 17592186523920 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523926 1069 "q-149461" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523926 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523926 1071 "In summer, the daily maximum temperature in a particular city (rounded to the nearest degree) varies between ${25}^{∘}C$ and ${36}^{∘}C,$ inclusive. Assuming that each maximum temperature value has the same probability of occurring on a given summer day, what is the expected value of the daily maximum temperature?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523926 1072 #uuid "061c757d-590b-5605-a6a4-5e9539c29dc6" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523926 1074 "Let $X$ represent the daily maximum temperature (rounded to the nearest degree) on a randomly selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{25+36}{2}=30.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the maximum temperature is ${30.5}^{∘}C.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523927 1068 17592186523854 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523927 1069 "q-342365" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523927 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523927 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(4,-4).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523927 1072 #uuid "08550377-8abe-5e0e-9fca-2cd79ce06f39" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523927 1074 "Let $f(x)=-2\\sqrt{x}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(4)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the coordinates of our point $(4,-4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523928 1068 17592186523921 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523928 1069 "q-167073" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523928 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523928 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523928 1072 #uuid "0bc94237-c7e5-59e2-b81f-4ca609b2837a" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523928 1074 "Previously, we found that $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has equation\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & =-x+7 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=7.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(7,0).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523929 1068 17592186523833 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523929 1069 "q-121687" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523929 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523929 1071 "![](images/d3/d330e7c959ede9f969573752b9f67e3ce73c732a680b58cd2f9f466f1cf5db4c.png)\n\nA conical container is $21\\,\\text{ft}$ tall with a diameter of $14\\,\\text{ft}.$ The container is being emptied so that the liquid level lowers at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ At what rate is the liquid volume decreasing when the liquid level is $16\\,\\text{ft}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523929 1072 #uuid "0c589335-1cfb-54d8-9a5c-d454270a6ef5" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523929 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=21\\,\\text{ft}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{14}{2}=7\\,\\text{ft}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the liquid level. We call $h$ the height of the liquid and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the liquid. Both of these quantities, and the liquid volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the liquid must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{7}{21} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (16)^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-56.9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the liquid volume decreases at a rate of approximately $56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523930 1068 17592186523842 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523930 1069 "q-216294" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523930 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523930 1071 "![](images/94/941efa39e90b946e68ea9ccc65ab7b97f4fce84d1cccbaa19135f8c87383a22f.png)\n\nThe exact length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523930 1072 #uuid "10bae273-6993-5b75-9a9e-139dcefc8633" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523930 1074 "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(-2,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(2,7)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-2))^{2}+(7-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523931 1068 17592186523793 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523931 1069 "q-86335" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523931 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523931 1071 "![](images/ce/ce513880934a99b289c22f734e8a455c3534bddd911968cd06b7fe88f65441aa.png)\n\nA hot air balloon $B$ is flying horizontally to the observer $O$ at a constant height of $300\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the balloon and an observer is increasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the balloon when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523931 1072 #uuid "14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523931 1074 "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523932 1068 17592186523915 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523932 1069 "q-97661" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523932 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523932 1071 "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $70$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store does not have the first $10$ issues in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the expected value of the chosen issue?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523932 1072 #uuid "17dce6e5-6992-5bd8-99ae-27a5145f8ae5" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523932 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the selected issue. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{11,12…,70\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{11,12,…,70\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11+70}{2}=40.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen number is $40.5.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523933 1068 17592186523828 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523933 1069 "q-122989" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523933 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523933 1071 "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[-4,-2].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523933 1072 #uuid "1bfa03d7-9352-51b2-9697-231f8df40ac5" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523933 1074 "We can simplify the given summation as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}+{z}_{0})Δ{z}_{1}+({z}_{2}+{z}_{1})Δ{z}_{2}+⋯+({z}_{q}+{z}_{q-1})Δ{z}_{n} \\\\  & =({z}_{1}+{z}_{0})({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}+{z}_{1})({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+⋯+({z}_{q}+{z}_{q-1})({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =({z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{0}^{2})+({z}_{2}^{2}-{z}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}^{2}-{z}_{q-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}^{2}}-{z}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}^{2}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}^{2}+{z}_{q}^{2} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}^{2}-{z}_{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${z}_{0}=-4$ and ${z}_{q}=-2$ are the endpoints of our partition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k} & ={z}_{q}^{2}-{z}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523934 1068 17592186523884 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523934 1069 "q-143618" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523934 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523934 1071 "![](images/48/483c019e9c68f857379192d2b40ab84803c4360ca4e0158adc0a8a3bc1636c4e.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523934 1072 #uuid "1c8173cc-49c9-5f6d-b758-9380f0f1613b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523934 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/ff/ffba85fc6ae71a1a5c250a90d686e8852c77756eebd228f3d3a2a9622cba01e1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523935 1068 17592186523841 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523935 1069 "q-71580" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523935 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523935 1071 "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{8}{x-2y+3z}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523935 1072 #uuid "1d879b56-35ea-5e82-93d4-20d74ef5904c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523935 1074 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{8}{x-2y+3z} & =c \\\\ 8 & =c(x-2y+3z) \\\\ cx-2cy+3cz-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be zero. Therefore, we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\ncx-2cy+3cz-8=0,\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523936 1068 17592186523859 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523936 1069 "q-97677" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523936 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523936 1071 "A traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $6$th and before the $15$th of September. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Assuming that each day after the $6$th and before the $15$th of September has the same probability of being chosen, what is the standard deviation of the selected days? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523936 1072 #uuid "1e997cfb-3847-52d2-9127-67ab63d15dde" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523936 1074 "Let $X$ represent the date of the selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,8,…,14\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =14-7+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{7,8,…,14\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{8}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{63}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{63}{12}}≈2.29.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523937 1068 17592186523804 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523937 1069 "q-293702" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523937 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523937 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+2}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523937 1072 #uuid "1f231ab0-36cf-59c7-86c7-11a5d98e603e" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523937 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3}-2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3}-2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{(\\sqrt{3}+2)(\\sqrt{3}-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}-10-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-(2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{3}-10-3}{3-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{3}-13}{-1} & = \\\\ 13-7\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523938 1068 17592186523820 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523938 1069 "q-97687" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523938 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523938 1071 "Lucy's marbles are labeled with numbers $0$ through $9.$ If Lucy chooses a marble at random, what is the standard deviation of the numbers of the selected marbles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523938 1072 #uuid "2200f2ed-00f4-5dc2-a53c-43c556e4cd84" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523938 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,9\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =9-0+1 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,9\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{10}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{99}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{99}{12}}≈2.87.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523939 1068 17592186523900 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523939 1069 "q-342351" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523939 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523939 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}$ at the point  $(2,1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523939 1072 #uuid "25f1b261-6dfa-5b14-a68c-dd89758e1b4c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523939 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(2)$, which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2}^{3}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-2) \\\\ y & =2x-4+1 \\\\ y & =2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523940 1068 17592186523886 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523940 1069 "q-95587" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523940 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523940 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=x{sech}^{2}⁡x.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523940 1072 #uuid "26dd88c1-a8ad-595c-af19-c13d906feda9" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523940 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={sech}^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(x)}^{′}\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot {({sech}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x-2x{sech}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523941 1068 17592186523901 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523941 1069 "q-143717" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523941 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523941 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523941 1072 #uuid "27951774-1d49-5cba-9914-8523d1f1e8cb" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523941 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/f5/f5ceaa24d74de28cc24de05ab352fbe4586c4c83630d6163f4871ccbe74e2cd2.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(1+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[xv+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+{x}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523942 1068 17592186523898 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523942 1069 "q-122969" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523942 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523942 1071 "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[2,8].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523942 1072 #uuid "28824d9b-0c0b-596c-9f64-75c745081e48" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523942 1074 "Note that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[2,8],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523943 1068 17592186523909 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523943 1069 "q-143003" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523943 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523943 1071 "![](images/3a/3abecc8d3b60b6c9796fc9c9449888c700d205aad90786fa9e4fee854cdc07e1.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{BO}?$\n\nI. $\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}-\\overset{\\to }{OD}$\n\nII. $\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC}$\n\nIII. $\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523943 1072 #uuid "2ccb8873-58fb-5db3-858e-4560c5c188b7" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523943 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}-\\overset{\\to }{OD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD})+\\overset{\\to }{DO} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DO} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BO}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{CO} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BO}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BO}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523944 1068 17592186523821 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523944 1069 "q-143707" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523944 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523944 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,4)=1,\\,F(5,1)=\\frac{2}{9},\\,F(4,4)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,F(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523944 1072 #uuid "2ebdf296-e322-505e-9c4f-5149cacb6a7e" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523944 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523945 1068 17592186523805 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523945 1069 "q-6929" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523945 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523945 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523945 1072 #uuid "350d70bb-36ec-51d9-b44c-9369bfdb5b19" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523945 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2x+1)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x+2)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions  $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1068 17592186523815 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1068 17592186523881 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1069 "q-167074" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1071 "Write the equation of $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ in **standard form**.\n\nThe equation is \n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$y=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1072 #uuid "3fd08c56-40e8-5db2-852c-0b5043fc5d70" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523946 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+y & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x+3y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\nx+3y=7.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523947 1068 17592186523887 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523947 1069 "q-143703" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523947 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523947 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,5)=0.63,\\,F(0,5)=0.5,\\,F(-2,5)=0.27.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523947 1072 #uuid "419b7449-2db5-5f6c-9da4-4ea57433c3f1" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523947 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523948 1068 17592186523824 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523948 1069 "q-2910" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523948 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523948 1071 "![](images/a5/a51d0d8ef1be2e7fc06aa34fe1440e77a79d8bee654703c1e931332390741c32.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ ladder leans against the side of a building, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. Given that the foot of the ladder $F$ is being pulled away from the building at the rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/s}$, at what rate is $\\theta $ decreasing at the moment when the top of the ladder $T$ is $12\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523948 1072 #uuid "419bbacd-9dab-5629-96fc-19c329c4d054" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523948 1074 "![](images/7b/7b923b76e90d665a722643684ce94fff50500a7be8b70aa821410a7969e4bb79.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the foot of the ladder to the wall of the building, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the ladder. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the ladder's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=12$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{26} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\cdot \\frac{13}{12}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\theta $ is *decreasing* at a rate of $\\frac{1}{24}$ radians per second." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523949 1068 17592186523803 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523949 1069 "q-143308" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523949 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523949 1071 "A poll of $800$ randomly selected U.S. Internet subscribers reveals that only $560$ of those interviewed are satisfied with their current internet provider. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. internet users that are satisfied with their current internet providers.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523949 1072 #uuid "42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523949 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. internet users,\n\n- some internet subscribers are satisfied with their current internet provider, and some aren't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=800,$ and\n\n- the proportion of Internet subscribers from the sample who are satisfied with their current internet provider is found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{560}{800}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=800\\cdot 0.7=560>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=800\\cdot (1-0.7)=240>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{800}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1068 17592186523802 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1068 17592186523806 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1069 "q-167071" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1071 "Find the $y$-intercept of $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ and express the equation of this line in slope-intercept form.\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the line is $b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n\nThe equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1072 #uuid "4a3e6aca-bc4b-546e-bf1b-908fe05212b5" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523950 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nAs we found previously, the slope is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+b \\\\ 1 & =-(\\frac{1}{3})(4)+b \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{4}{3}+b \\\\ 1+\\frac{4}{3} & =b \\\\ b & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523951 1068 17592186523845 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523951 1069 "q-143739" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523951 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523951 1071 "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523951 1072 #uuid "4bec72aa-1d05-5e53-b2ca-0df4a3dfbe51" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523951 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a5/a5f876c9351040845fcc5e6839f4ca776fe22988a5e4c814d4a7b063692f083c.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(0,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<0,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=0}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(0+\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523952 1068 17592186523838 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523952 1069 "q-97653" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523952 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523952 1071 "Ann chooses a card at random from a deck of cards labeled $28$ through $65.$  What is the expected value of the chosen number?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523952 1072 #uuid "4dac21a9-fd23-5a65-b9b0-0999f6a3ac35" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523952 1074 "Let $X$ represent the value of the number chosen. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{28,29,…,65\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{28,29,…,65\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{28+65}{2}=46.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen number is $46.5.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523953 1068 17592186523814 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523953 1069 "q-167069" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523953 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523953 1071 "What is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$\n\n$|\\overset{―}{AB}|\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523953 1072 #uuid "53008a1b-048b-5530-aee9-c37309ee0c8c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523953 1074 "From the picture, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{―}{AB}| & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-(-2))^{2}+(1-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523954 1068 17592186523873 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523954 1069 "q-143381" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523954 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523954 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=160$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $5%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523954 1072 #uuid "53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523954 1074 "Since $n=160$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=5%=0.05,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=160\\cdot 0.05=8>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=160\\cdot (1-0.05)=152>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.05\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{160}} \\\\  & 0.05\\pm 0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523955 1068 17592186523791 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523955 1069 "q-149510" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523955 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523955 1071 "A spinner divided into eight equal sections numbered $2$ through $9$ is spun. What is the variance of the numbers obtained?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523955 1072 #uuid "56f939dc-3462-5544-b92f-c768cb817339" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523955 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number obtained by spinning the spinner. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{2,3,…,9\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =9-2+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{2,3,…,9\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{8}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{63}{12}=5.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the numbers obtained is $5.25$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523956 1068 17592186523895 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523956 1069 "q-86352" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523956 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523956 1071 "![](images/8f/8f4638863dbc6bdfbff00d20d2153bb73eb7779719bbc7235d730539cb92329e.png)\n\nAngela observes that a drone is flying horizontally at a constant height of $5\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. If the drone is flying in the opposite direction from Angela's location at a speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ decreases at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523956 1072 #uuid "579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523956 1074 "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523957 1068 17592186523874 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523957 1069 "q-97695" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523957 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523957 1071 "In a video game, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $80$ and $125,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability of occurring, what is the standard deviation of the number of obstacles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523957 1072 #uuid "57d50a92-18de-56e3-a7c3-f6f999c0fbca" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523957 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =125-80+1 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{46}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2\\,115}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,115}{12}}≈13.28.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523958 1068 17592186523851 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523958 1069 "q-314884" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523958 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523958 1071 "![](images/bf/bf0aa0508025b800bb0707b74ffdc89b08052ad6fced401b8071d7ad741f8e0a.png)\n\nA lighthouse $L$ shines a beam that hits the surface of the sea at point $B.$ The light is mounted at a height of $10\\,\\text{m}$ above the water. As the beam is tilted downward, the angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the beam and the point $B$ is increasing at a rate of $0.12$ radians per second. What is the speed of the light spot as a function of $\\theta ?$ \n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\nThe speed of the light spot is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523958 1072 #uuid "5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523958 1074 "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1068 17592186523860 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1068 17592186523870 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1069 "q-167067" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1071 "What are the $xy$-coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n$A\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1072 #uuid "5d427fb2-847d-5c45-9124-9759bd9b4b84" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523959 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$- and $y$-axes.\n\n* The point is $2$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, its $x$-coordinate is $-2.$\n\n* The point is $3$ units *above* the origin, in the *positive* direction of the $y$-axis. So, its $y$-coordinate is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of our point are $(-2,3).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523960 1068 17592186523857 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523960 1069 "q-143712" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523960 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523960 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523960 1072 #uuid "61f58f19-0648-54c0-b55e-2caca7ea1507" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523960 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/1f/1fbbe9e4728cd5aefcc456a6c8666e3e2e6c0d0bc832f471677d908cf6c012e3.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v(1-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}\\frac{2}{3}v\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=1}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1068 17592186523810 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1068 17592186523863 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1069 "q-167068" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1071 "Write the equations of the vertical and horizontal lines that pass through the point $B.$\n\nThe **equation** of the vertical line is \n{{field-1}}\n\nThe **equation** of the horizontal line is \n{{field-2}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1072 #uuid "6b73135a-679b-5521-bab1-ffd76e8e9cf8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523961 1074 "The coordinates of $B$ are $(4,1).$\n\n* Since the vertical line should pass through $(4,1)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $4.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\n* Since the horizontal line should pass through $(4,1)$, the $y$-value for every point on the line is $1.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1068 17592186523795 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1068 17592186523865 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1069 "q-278259" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1071 "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1072 #uuid "6baac323-becf-503f-bb38-28a603f7f3c3" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523962 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x+1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523963 1068 17592186523897 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523963 1069 "q-121763" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523963 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523963 1071 "![](images/22/2264aa2a4f3d33b04190608ab638604dbe09dfd278035b2b764aa3d85d947624.png)\n\nGreta is flying a kite $K.$ The kite is  $150\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and moving horizontally towards Greta at a speed of $10\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523963 1072 #uuid "6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523963 1074 "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523964 1068 17592186523832 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523964 1069 "q-53161" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523964 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523964 1071 "![](images/06/0640883625711f20e1441946461abd0f352ed1418065b325f48ea2b709fef230.png)\n\nA cableway system is descending at an angle of ${45}^{∘},$ and the speed of its cabin $C$ is $10\\,\\text{mi/h}.$ The cabin casts a shadow $S$ vertically downwards to the ground. Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, find the rate at which the distance between the cableway's shadow to the landing point $L$ is decreasing." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523964 1072 #uuid "7145361b-50f5-5e70-a538-4868d130565b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523964 1074 "![](images/4d/4d2a9ab6bc60aa5733be4dc29aea2b03d16edbebed371963b795297a4c79a676.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance from the cableway's shadow to the landing point, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the cabin, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,\\text{mi/h}$ (negative because the distance between the cableway's shadow and the landing point is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-10)=-5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal distance is decreasing at a rate of $5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523965 1068 17592186523891 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523965 1069 "q-184137" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523965 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523965 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Nine fewer than a number.\"\n\nUse the letter $p$ to represent the unknown number.\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523965 1072 #uuid "71607034-c109-5c32-ac61-2615649524b9" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523965 1074 "If we denote our number by $p,$ then the phrase \"nine fewer than a number\" can be written as \n\n$$\np-9.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523966 1068 17592186523801 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523966 1069 "q-2840" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523966 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523966 1071 "![](images/77/7732722fc875e8cf13482394990baa0b5f8306a8151fca934491bb12dd181d9e.png)\n\nA  $10\\,\\text{cm}$ tall funnel containing water is being drained at a constant rate of  $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The mouth of the funnel is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter. How fast is the water level dropping when there is $15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of water remaining in the funnel?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523966 1072 #uuid "734a1589-9b26-5ca8-b559-8254d0411564" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523966 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=10\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=\\frac{12}{2}=6\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pi  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =125 \\\\ h & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ({5}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =9\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523967 1068 17592186523839 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523967 1069 "q-69510" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523967 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523967 1071 "![](images/4b/4b441f7b59f59240141380711d5d380ea58cafa01f8a288f1e5c8bc7685650cf.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=b,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ in terms of $a,b$ and $c.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523967 1072 #uuid "73e1cb61-57dc-5ce3-bb9e-62d28b4019a8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523967 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $P$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\overset{\\to }{PS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bf9ce3f20027a3ce358f8a0a9d72107f087021ca698e677df367e05a7c3e5bb.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n \n\n![](images/6b/6b37dcafa2b2b913d7e5328e19257c4d873c5877186df24626033fec8216da05.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ} \\\\  & =a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523968 1068 17592186523844 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523968 1069 "q-344973" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523968 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523968 1071 "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume increase when height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523968 1072 #uuid "748701f6-8780-55dc-bf9a-808d48cf28f4" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523968 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523969 1068 17592186523906 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523969 1069 "q-350260" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523969 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523969 1071 "Olivia and Harper are musicians. In the previous season, the decimal proportion of Olivia’s performances that were solos was $0.55,$ while $40%$ of Harper’s performances were solos. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\nII. More than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\nIII. Olivia had a greater percentage of solo performances compared to Harper." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523969 1072 #uuid "76598254-2f11-555f-8a0a-b51f61e7748b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523969 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.55>0.5,\n$$\n\nmore than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n40%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $0.55$ and $40%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.55\\times 100=55%\n$$\n\nSince $55%>40%,$ Olivia had a **greater** percentage of solo performances compared to Harper.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523970 1068 17592186523840 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523970 1069 "q-47412" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523970 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523970 1071 "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523970 1072 #uuid "77bfba8d-0add-517b-b669-b916e6dff897" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523970 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{9}{(1)^{4}}=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523971 1068 17592186523902 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523971 1069 "q-122994" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523971 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523971 1071 "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[1,10].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}2({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523971 1072 #uuid "785f4fec-653e-532c-a878-3835d225527c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523971 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}2({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{m} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${x}_{0}=1$ and ${x}_{m}=10$ are the endpoints of our partition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={10}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}=2\\cdot 99=198.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1068 17592186523867 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1068 17592186523869 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1068 17592186523872 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1068 17592186523876 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1068 17592186523908 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1068 17592186523916 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1069 "q-334286" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1071 "![](images/1e/1e116b0a2ba3678cde3b321b23500f532037b0cafd3842be008ee8abc3c0ee28.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-5}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\nHence, \n{{field-6}}." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1072 #uuid "78d32ec9-eea0-570e-81c9-743259524853" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523972 1074 "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523973 1068 17592186523905 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523973 1069 "q-314057" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523973 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523973 1071 "The volume of a cone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always one third of its height.  Find the rate of the cone's height increase when the  height is $6\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523973 1072 #uuid "7cce7dd9-a52f-5429-96fd-d564ac81f3b8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523973 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is one third of the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ 2r & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{6}h)}^{2}h=\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's height is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\text{ m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523974 1068 17592186523885 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523974 1069 "q-51396" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523974 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523974 1071 "![](images/86/8676d9a562d254a1194502c5a45368d5345f81aee3a066c28acc87cb1f33812c.png)\n\nThe height of an ice cream cone is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$ Ice cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice cream level is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice cream level decreasing at that moment?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523974 1072 #uuid "7d9fecf0-f483-51c1-b6ab-f1ee3ed71254" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523974 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=12\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{6})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi ({6}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523975 1068 17592186523913 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523975 1069 "q-143657" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523975 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523975 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523975 1072 #uuid "7ed10907-9632-53b1-9eec-d2c7cfd4e80a" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523975 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4f/4f5cfe5e819f9479199ba1af6116627b31d03c88d8aa4c6e049bb375316c2c10.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523976 1068 17592186523896 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523976 1069 "q-343159" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523976 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523976 1071 "![](images/55/55e96238e8263a1744c117bad09d1b11947f70a8bf1b837c52af18cf2e244ab7.png)\n\nA park ranger $P$ stands $900\\,\\text{ft}$ away from the base of a lookout tower $T$ to track a drone $D$ that is ascending vertically. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the ranger and the drone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ radians per second. At what rate is the drone gaining altitude at the moment when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone’s altitude is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523976 1072 #uuid "82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523976 1074 "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523977 1068 17592186523792 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523977 1069 "q-2558" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523977 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523977 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c0d78ce05859db77dcc1e5198596a1a02c798c607ba9c4ff78e6fe1362b113.png)\n\nThe height of a conical cup is $6\\text{ cm}$ and the opening radius is $2\\text{ cm}.$ Water leaks out of the bottom at the rate of $2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If the water level is $3\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the water level decreasing at that moment?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523977 1072 #uuid "829ceea0-b4f0-5bef-b797-d4b161d8e247" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523977 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cup, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi ({3}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2 & =\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523978 1068 17592186523868 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523978 1069 "q-122930" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523978 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523978 1071 "Which of the following are partitions of the real interval $[4,8]?$\n\nI. $4<6<8$\n\nII. $5<6<7<8$\n\nIII. $4<5<6<7$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523978 1072 #uuid "84a42ed7-7cdb-52df-a889-40521b20e8c1" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523978 1074 "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement I is a partition of $[4,8].$\n\n- Statement II is not a partition of $[4,8]$ since it does not include the left endpoint $a=4.$\n\n- Statement III is not a partition of $[4,8]$ since it does not include the right endpoint $b=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523979 1068 17592186523847 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523979 1069 "q-101924" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523979 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523979 1071 "![](images/72/72cfa1c8918f9f50b2426eb05ac16eed5605f557b59870aa914d07148d782676.png)\n\nA point $Q$ is shown on the axis above. To which quadrant does it belong?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523979 1072 #uuid "84a619bd-064d-5238-b773-07631be56d2c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523979 1074 "We label the quadrants as follows:\n\n![](images/0a/0a6e04eba0886d64c1a36b259c7a3c17afb9a86ad5169e28f476fff2d31c68b1.png)\n\nSince $Q$ is placed below and to the left of the origin, it lies in quadrant $\\text{III},$ which is the third quadrant." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523980 1068 17592186523880 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523980 1069 "q-83679" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523980 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523980 1071 "![](images/d2/d2e2666baf2931dc4e0d8ba097a364b7b7a1bba75e65c8d802a60180e9a94854.png)\n\nNeah is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $112\\,\\text{ft}.$ The angle of elevation $\\theta $ of the kite is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{28}$ radians per second. Find, as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the kite's altitude is increasing." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523980 1072 #uuid "8512c354-4d05-539a-b5eb-ac69014916bb" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523980 1074 "![](images/8c/8c3c9e40f56d0b269562c37fd2d87c7e8f0394c8f55dca596e5e751f5e96157b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Neah. The quantity $z=112\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{112} \\\\ y & =112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{28}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\pi }{28} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the kite is gaining altitude is $4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523981 1068 17592186523899 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523981 1069 "q-80681" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523981 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523981 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523981 1072 #uuid "86d3c939-36b2-5329-b38e-94ddc558ef8b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523981 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}-x+2=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =1-8 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523982 1068 17592186523889 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523982 1069 "q-83846" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523982 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523982 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523982 1072 #uuid "893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523982 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=20.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523983 1068 17592186523907 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523983 1069 "q-122993" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523983 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523983 1071 "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[2,6].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\\frac{({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})}{2}Δ{z}_{k}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523983 1072 #uuid "8a05e0a6-f018-5eeb-9fa0-754bde9f9f75" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523983 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\\frac{({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})}{2}Δ{z}_{k}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the summation as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}+{z}_{0})Δ{z}_{1}+({z}_{2}+{z}_{1})Δ{z}_{2}+⋯+({z}_{q}+{z}_{q-1})Δ{z}_{n} \\\\  & =({z}_{1}+{z}_{0})({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}+{z}_{1})({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+⋯+({z}_{q}+{z}_{q-1})({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =({z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{0}^{2})+({z}_{2}^{2}-{z}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}^{2}-{z}_{q-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}^{2}}-{z}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}^{2}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}^{2}+{z}_{q}^{2} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}^{2}-{z}_{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${z}_{0}=2$ and ${z}_{q}=6$ are the endpoints of our partition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k} & ={z}_{q}^{2}-{z}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2}-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 32=16.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523984 1068 17592186523914 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523984 1069 "q-128553" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523984 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523984 1071 "Which of the following are natural numbers?\n\nI. $-1$\n\nII. $0$\n\nIII. $11$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523984 1072 #uuid "8ba50b21-9ef4-5151-a99b-e87a06229c81" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523984 1074 "The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers.\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…\n$$\n\nOf the given numbers, $11$ is a natural number, but $-1$ and $0$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1068 17592186523830 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1068 17592186523912 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1069 "q-342378" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point  $(-1,-2)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1072 #uuid "8bcad8aa-5196-5826-97b4-5ca478a3c38f" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523985 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =1\\cdot [x-(-1)] \\\\ y+2 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+1-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0-1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-1).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523986 1068 17592186523831 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523986 1069 "q-332559" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523986 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523986 1071 "![](images/5b/5b65d3044591af808c6f27f54e7bfb723b053b66c335929e96c18624c94233b3.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{BA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{FA}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{FE}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{CB}$ in terms of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{CB}=$   \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523986 1072 #uuid "95308c1b-5af9-5c02-9df1-10249ec99ede" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523986 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $C$ to $B$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}+\\overset{\\to }{FA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18b8d39937dd23f03a030727e642729259ec5ab0c6fcb438c8bf82a2b7aaf08d.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CE}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{EF}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{FA}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}=-a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/6f/6f1c084747ad1b0de2ab28979fee8e14db9463f1d82d07ab787581789bcb6e37.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}+\\overset{\\to }{FA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =d-c+b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523987 1068 17592186523817 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523987 1069 "q-95575" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523987 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523987 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=3{csch}^{2}⁡x.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523987 1072 #uuid "9541bdac-540f-537e-a50e-b6a223ada95f" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523987 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=3{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6g(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523988 1068 17592186523823 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523988 1069 "q-167075" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523988 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523988 1071 "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,-2)$ and is parallel to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523988 1072 #uuid "9a61b0f7-d3d4-5914-af7a-56cdc12fc887" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523988 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ that passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-2).$ \n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-3) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523989 1068 17592186523800 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523989 1069 "q-121586" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523989 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523989 1071 "![](images/80/8036801735554767a33bdee0ff96a95d7e44bc3f97bacb396ec0de6d38f4aafc.png)\n\nA cone is being filled with ice-cream at a rate of $4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The height of the cone is always three times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the ice cream volume increasing when the radius is $1\\,\\text{cm}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523989 1072 #uuid "9a637ece-bf3b-5e1e-8874-577affcbf215" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523989 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the ice cream volume and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base diameter. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =3\\pi (1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4 & =3\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523990 1068 17592186523808 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523990 1069 "q-121577" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523990 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523990 1071 "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6.25\\,\\text{ft}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523990 1072 #uuid "9c01ea5e-36e4-5dc4-b2f4-1bae38b20756" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523990 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6.25\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{3}{5}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{5}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi (6.25)^{2}(24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523991 1068 17592186523836 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523991 1069 "q-236833" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523991 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523991 1071 "$23-48=${{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523991 1072 #uuid "9d2a64c1-9bb3-5573-99a2-5c763e9021bd" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523991 1074 "Since $48$ is larger than $23,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n48-23\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n48-23=25.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n23-48=-25.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523992 1068 17592186523862 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523992 1069 "q-119011" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523992 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523992 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(-4\\sqrt{5},-2\\sqrt{5})$ and $(\\sqrt{5},-12\\sqrt{5})?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523992 1072 #uuid "9dd278e6-83f6-5c4b-b1f1-6a6ae9e2e467" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523992 1074 "In this case, we have ${{(-4\\sqrt{5},-2\\sqrt{5})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(\\sqrt{5},-12\\sqrt{5})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{5}-(-4\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}+{(-12\\sqrt{5}-(-2\\sqrt{5}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(-10\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(5)+100(5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125+500} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{625} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523993 1068 17592186523827 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523993 1069 "q-346630" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523993 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523993 1071 "![](images/e5/e56e31d999674075f3af880e8eadf14cd978f2dba73dd946dc103aae298f6681.png)\n\nAn inverted right circular conical tank contains sand. The height of the tank is $24\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter of its top base is $16\\,\\text{in}.$ The sand is draining from the bottom of the tank so that the height of the sand, $h,$ is decreasing at a constant rate of $6\\,\\text{in/h}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of sand is decreasing when the height of the sand is $9\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nHint: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe sand volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523993 1072 #uuid "9ddf5a2d-9bc3-5a54-8df4-57b5fda24b44" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523993 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=24\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical tank, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top base. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the sand level. We call $h$ the height of the sand pile and $r$ the radius of the top surface of the sand. Both of these quantities, and the sand volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the remaining sand must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{24} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of sand is changing when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (9)^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sand volume is decreasing at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1068 17592186523826 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1068 17592186523843 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1069 "q-308821" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,1)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1072 #uuid "a153fbfd-b7f1-5770-9660-ea9e18ac215d" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523994 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,4,0\\,).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523995 1068 17592186523903 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523995 1069 "q-143004" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523995 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523995 1071 "![](images/b0/b0009b057eef7d5d4e380883f26442b2c4e5777e90c77a7fd7791506c87cbcd4.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDE$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{BE}?$\n\nI. $\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}$\n\nII. $\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{DE}$\n\nIII. $\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523995 1072 #uuid "a225df54-1e5c-59a8-9085-a55a59c0af5b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523995 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE} & =(\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA})+\\overset{\\to }{AE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CE} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BE}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD})+\\overset{\\to }{DE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BE}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE} & =\\overset{\\to }{BE}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523996 1068 17592186523890 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523996 1069 "q-162798" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523996 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523996 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523996 1072 #uuid "a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523996 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523997 1068 17592186523809 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523997 1069 "q-70552" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523997 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523997 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523997 1072 #uuid "a5878b34-7fd0-5b9f-89d8-91c764ed89b8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523997 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is the only vertical asymptote." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523998 1068 17592186523919 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523998 1069 "q-344979" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523998 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523998 1071 "The height of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume decrease when the height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer. \n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523998 1072 #uuid "a8aac6cd-427e-5b7e-aea2-44c84e419786" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523998 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523999 1068 17592186523853 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523999 1069 "q-85400" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523999 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523999 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $9?$\n\nI. $7\\,050\\,113$\n\nII. $5\\,119\\,232$\n\nIII. $14\\,624\\,100$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523999 1072 #uuid "a8e40c21-fa80-544c-8d3a-4f03c8add411" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186523999 1074 "A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $7\\,050\\,113$ is\n\n$$\n7+0+5+0+1+1+3=17,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $9.$ So, $7\\,050\\,113$ is *not* divisible by $9.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $5\\,119\\,232$ is\n\n$$\n5+1+1+9+2+3+2=23,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $9.$ So, $5\\,119\\,232$ is *not* divisible by $9.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $14\\,624\\,100$ is\n\n$$\n1+4+6+2+4+1+0+0=18,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $9.$ So, $14\\,624\\,100$ is divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"llI only.\"" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524000 1068 17592186523837 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524000 1069 "q-2858" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524000 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524000 1071 "![](images/51/51ee41eea6dfef1292c68a13a438f43c327639e01452e29998bbe513ac3838ba.png)\n\nA container has the shape of a right circular cone. The height of the container is $20\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter at the top of the container is $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Water in the container is evaporating so that its depth, $h$, is decreasing at a constant rate of $\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in/h}.$ At what rate is the volume of water decreasing when the water depth is $12\\,\\text{in}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524000 1072 #uuid "a9bed4a9-ed2b-5603-85e6-df6b8285a648" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524000 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=20\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the container is changing when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi (12)^{2}(-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is decreasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524001 1068 17592186523819 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524001 1069 "q-328092" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524001 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524001 1071 "![](images/4c/4c895d3f634102ff747342616f572b6a2fa8a57c86215f20b47d2187e6e7f96d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{DA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{AB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DE}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{DC}$ in terms of vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{DC}=$  \n {{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524001 1072 #uuid "ae6e233b-a8cc-54d7-a535-be00579ec6e5" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524001 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $D$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DC}=\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd94c2f43b5da5252879f8acdcc14fc2f951f9a899903482bd3947ca8ec6921e.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DA}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{BC}=c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/e0/e0f53eb04be491e8b84e2c7d5d252ab2c04bdfe372a93a503788a1ee94f63675.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC} & =\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =a+b+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524002 1068 17592186523864 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524002 1069 "q-293620" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524002 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524002 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524002 1072 #uuid "b0314c88-d178-5a7a-83d0-8641a658389f" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524002 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}-(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6-4\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6-12} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{(-6)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524003 1068 17592186523798 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524003 1069 "q-143693" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524003 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524003 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}({y}^{2}+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524003 1072 #uuid "b1b6ab32-2ee9-5286-82fb-e9633b19fc28" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524003 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfa6842026fc1635efe939525d60a9c83c89735a1a1e69d32d1b31c8431f02c.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{[\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2})]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{3}{5}(x{v}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{[\\frac{x{v}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524004 1068 17592186523825 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524004 1069 "q-10838" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524004 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524004 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}+5x$ at the point $(-1,-4).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524004 1072 #uuid "b262293b-c158-5061-ba4d-49a88b31e09c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524004 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5x)=2x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1)=2(-1)+5=3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=3$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y & =3x+3-4 \\\\ y & =3x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524005 1068 17592186523834 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524005 1069 "q-143656" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524005 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524005 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y<0.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524005 1072 #uuid "b7fd97ca-9b98-50c0-b683-53f1d4a2aa1d" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524005 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/1232f75c56b548896fc3c0371e36201eb24c71811d59d185440a964e393818be.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524006 1068 17592186523812 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524006 1069 "q-121579" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524006 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524006 1071 "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $7$ times its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $2\\,\\text{in}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524006 1072 #uuid "babb3260-0ebb-5378-81f6-fb356d66422e" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524006 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $7$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (7h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{49}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =49\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-14\\pi  & =49\\pi (2)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524007 1068 17592186523790 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524007 1069 "q-143742" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524007 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524007 1071 "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524007 1072 #uuid "c246e56e-54f4-5ef4-b7e3-0ed423a3b4da" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524007 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/53/53d0466b98c4b9b2704cc2f7610e7f28cee6ec27aaf2ea94be75c5b3f32a8257.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(1,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<1,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}[\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}3{v}^{2}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524008 1068 17592186523894 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524008 1069 "q-95567" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524008 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524008 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524008 1072 #uuid "c4169769-2e08-50ac-8627-9cd65f653a9b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524008 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g=4x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2{csch}^{2}\\,g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2{csch}^{2}⁡g\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524009 1068 17592186523797 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524009 1069 "q-121348" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524009 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524009 1071 "![](images/5e/5e96676aca66b99fd1e080d6c0cbbe79a5197125a3bcc8f04e67ca406947af10.png)\n\nA bullet $B$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ from the horizontal. Given that the bullet's horizontal speed is $300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ calculate the actual speed of the bullet in the direction it is traveling." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524009 1072 #uuid "c70a238b-9801-5892-a5eb-17f62e1925f8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524009 1074 "![](images/fe/fea05a36487813579d4633d9134bdd1d8c4d4bef4e9f57ba997b7b8715d46f32.png)\n\nLet $x$ denote the horizontal distance from the bullet's projection to the cannon, and $z$ be the distance between the bullet and the cannon, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=300\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2\\cdot 300=600.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the plane is $600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524010 1068 17592186523829 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524010 1069 "q-167076" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524010 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524010 1071 "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,8)$ and is perpendicular to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524010 1072 #uuid "c73684cd-cbd4-53ce-a02e-b8f120478413" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524010 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3},$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=3$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,8).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524011 1068 17592186523878 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524011 1069 "q-345004" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524011 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524011 1071 "![](images/a3/a3623402e5d5df9339f1a3bb615c92ff5fcff9ae8d96c3630c5cb847a3038eda.png)\n\nWater is leaking out of a cone-shaped container so that the volume of the water decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the container is three times its base radius. Find the rate of decrease of the radius of the water's surface when the radius is $6\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe radius is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524011 1072 #uuid "cc3b1d02-1e54-57cd-be11-d438beee9721" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524011 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the conical volume of water and $r$ be the radius of its surface. Both of these quantities, as well as the water's volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we want the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base radius. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =3\\pi (6)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -12 & =108\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the water's surface decreases at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524012 1068 17592186523850 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524012 1069 "q-103539" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524012 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524012 1071 "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}$ at the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524012 1072 #uuid "ccfa4d84-7836-5556-9446-f447928d0c91" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524012 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524013 1068 17592186523875 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524013 1069 "q-85403" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524013 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524013 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524013 1072 #uuid "cf9e8f5c-9d67-5259-afe2-aab0c57cf6c7" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524013 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{7-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}+2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524014 1068 17592186523855 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524014 1069 "q-97662" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524014 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524014 1071 "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $55$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store does not have the first $7$ issues in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the standard deviation of the issue numbers he could select? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524014 1072 #uuid "d0deae3f-c105-5c17-970a-aab6db3301cd" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524014 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{8,9,…,55\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =55-8+1 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{8,9,…,55\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{48}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2303}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2303}{12}}≈13.85.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524015 1068 17592186523892 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524015 1069 "q-121683" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524015 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524015 1071 "![](images/f9/f9998b94f05eb6345d375f35f4abc7f88edfc223c4a1af5e7bbc66c4b2a2f0bc.png)\n\nA conical paper cup is $15\\,\\text{m}$ tall with a diameter of $16\\,\\text{m}.$ The cup is being filled with water so that the water level rises at a rate of $1\\,\\text{m/h}.$ At what rate is the water volume increasing when the water level is $10\\,\\text{m}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524015 1072 #uuid "d2f18815-e582-5459-b43c-b02a5b4a0da8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524015 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=15\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the conical cup, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{15} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8}{15}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{8}{15}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi (10)^{2}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈28.4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the water volume increases at a rate of $28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524016 1068 17592186523861 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524016 1069 "q-143740" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524016 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524016 1071 "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524016 1072 #uuid "d405caca-881b-5a05-9419-094e813c3d9c" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524016 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/dd/dd7024bcd41ce8312f484763e0988ebcceb54e7154cd8ee3c209991bce5727c1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<-\\frac{3}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524017 1068 17592186523856 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524017 1069 "q-59888" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524017 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524017 1071 "![](images/dd/dd9943f680dedd516084909e74d769e7605c7180f04d55b3c12113834bb47136.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of rectangles to the total number of shapes?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524017 1072 #uuid "d65e17ea-2def-584a-904b-9397e33a3b17" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524017 1074 "We count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $3$ rectangles.\n\nWe count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $6$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to total shapes is $3:6.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of rectangles to the total shapes is $1:2.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524018 1068 17592186523816 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524018 1069 "q-85828" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524018 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524018 1071 "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ The radius of the cone is always equal to its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524018 1072 #uuid "d9e85e6a-5a02-52f7-ba3c-489084bc4a6f" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524018 1074 "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is equal to its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-72 & =\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524019 1068 17592186523807 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524019 1069 "q-167070" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524019 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524019 1071 "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n$m\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524019 1072 #uuid "db023775-f57a-5eec-8870-ad9e9767c00a" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524019 1074 "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524020 1068 17592186523799 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524020 1069 "q-121673" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524020 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524020 1071 "![](images/cf/cf9fda11d138a5227307070d52fc0a27ce9decc35c91efbb026b89d4507f2ce7.png)\n\nA beverage is pumped into a conical dispenser at a constant rate of  $19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the tank is $70\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter and the height of the tank is $40\\,\\text{cm}.$ How fast is the beverage level increasing when the volume of the beverage in the dispenser is $12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524020 1072 #uuid "e05d5196-22d0-5390-9d3c-413318bc7364" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524020 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=40\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the tank, and $R=\\frac{70}{2}=35\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the solution level. We call $h$ the solution level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the solution. Both of these quantities, and the solution volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the solution must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{35}{40} \\\\ r & =\\frac{7h}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{7h}{8})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of the beverage in the dispenser is changing when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.25\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =48 \\\\ h & =2\\sqrt[3]{6}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi (2\\sqrt[3]{6})^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49\\sqrt[3]{36}}{16}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & ≈1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the solution level is increasing at a rate of approximately $1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524021 1068 17592186523917 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524021 1069 "q-71526" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524021 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524021 1071 "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-7x$ at the point $(-1,5).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524021 1072 #uuid "e108591f-1dbb-58f4-aca2-6f29ab0389ab" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524021 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-7x)=6{x}^{2}-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(-1)=6(-1)^{2}-7=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-1)}=1.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524022 1068 17592186523811 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524022 1069 "q-122934" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524022 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524022 1071 "Which of the following are partitions of the real interval $[0,6]?$\n\nI. $0<2<4<8$\n\nII. $\\{0,3,6\\}$\n\nIII. $0<1<3<5<6$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524022 1072 #uuid "e49f5076-911d-5cdf-8140-a000de972953" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524022 1074 "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement I is not a partition of $[0,6]$ since $8$ does not belong to $[0,6].$\n\n- Statement II is a partition of $[0,6].$\n\n- Statement III is a partition of $[0,6].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524023 1068 17592186523835 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524023 1069 "q-346239" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524023 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524023 1071 "![](images/01/01d6575ff056e7f99075597547583b4c648fbe6ad462d38a5d5fd76b8c73a564.png)\n\nA conical container has a height of $8\\,\\text{cm},$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Batter is being poured into the container. If the level of the batter in the container is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}$, find the rate at which the volume of the batter is increasing when the radius of the batter surface is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of batter is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524023 1072 #uuid "e4ae8f6c-f256-5ee8-80b1-72f1337deae2" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524023 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=8\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=4\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the batter level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface, $r$ the radius of the cone at the surface of the batter, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface decreases as we pour the batter into the container.\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the batter must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{h}{2} \\\\ h & =3\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(-\\frac{4}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of batter is increasing at a rate of $9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524024 1068 17592186523866 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524024 1069 "q-143716" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524024 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524024 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2}+y, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524024 1072 #uuid "e52f8c43-5915-56b1-841a-9a33ac8f7470" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524024 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/12e26559137d7c494c5ddc6f1add55f646dd84236f355221042448ddeacd2879.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{x}u{v}^{2}+v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=1}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+xv-\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}-v]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{v}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)}{2}+v(x-1)]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}({x}^{2}-1)}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{2}(x-1)}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{6}+\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524025 1068 17592186523813 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524025 1069 "q-143695" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524025 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524025 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}x{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524025 1072 #uuid "e58401f9-a4c1-5465-9216-a1679c479a59" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524025 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/e4/e4aef49bebaf250fa93e0ad1daccbad324bf714c7e5627f5245ea8387bc8da87.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{12}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}u\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524026 1068 17592186523818 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524026 1069 "q-143701" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524026 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524026 1071 "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(8,10)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,F(8,8)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,F(8,4)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524026 1072 #uuid "e662cd14-0105-5064-96eb-7a2f86c8b78d" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524026 1074 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524027 1068 17592186523893 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524027 1069 "q-53196" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524027 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524027 1071 "![](images/d4/d486581ea48bc96adc1269fbf40d1d71a547e92977f71e3a74336a147ef9b222.png)\n\nA potato $P$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians from the horizontal. The horizontal distance the projectile travels increases at a rate of $18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ If the angle of elevation does not change, what is the speed of the potato?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524027 1072 #uuid "e817aceb-adb1-5216-bfe1-e48f45d3b767" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524027 1074 "![](images/8b/8b5af04f58fe65b46adaae47d97b857d1e8765122a811179cf25ba22733e6415.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the horizontal distance from the potato to the cannon and $z$ be the actual distance between them, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 18=12\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the potato is $12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524028 1068 17592186523888 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524028 1069 "q-83678" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524028 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524028 1071 "![](images/6a/6a37ae024b0472dfc38ecff01b898d8d3ed2384d90acf432d747efee1568520e.png)\n\nA car $C$ drives toward a $108\\,\\text{m}$ high building $B$ following a straight path. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the car and the top of the building $T$ is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ radians per second. What is the speed of the car when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524028 1072 #uuid "e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524028 1074 "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi (\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524029 1068 17592186523846 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524029 1069 "q-149467" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524029 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524029 1071 "In a math test, students have to answer a multiple-choice question, where the answers are labeled from $0$ to $6.$ If Steph selects an answer at random, what is the expected value of her chosen answer?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524029 1072 #uuid "e9910a94-d71d-54e3-8cb5-b52ba8bdfed9" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524029 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{0+6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen answer is $3.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524030 1068 17592186523858 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524030 1069 "q-335834" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524030 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524030 1071 "![](images/83/837da5064b1f09449bf10caa6738dbe276d71e922a5a4fa96566010fb8ae320a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{AE}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{DB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{CB}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{EC}$ in terms of  $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{EC}=$   \n {{field-1}}" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524030 1072 #uuid "eccc4fe9-82af-5d0c-a3d1-d04e0240d4c4" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524030 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $E$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{EC}=\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8b76bf9fc26742d8ba05c962e59986e9f7b596f0b02c8e71c4ead4b45cf542a.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{EA}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AD}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{DB}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{BC}=-c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/fe/fe663b4bfacc4ef2078e014af664e99123cb803fd26d03a449bf9bf6198629cc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{EC} & =\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =-a+d+b+(-c) \\\\  & =-a+d+b-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524031 1068 17592186523910 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524031 1069 "q-343202" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524031 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524031 1071 "![](images/30/305398b239bbc784fb5e5a1ec7ef2020a19a033dcda150e84f9c4a2a72a96adf.png)\n\nA lifeguard $L$ is watching a weather balloon $W$ rise vertically from a point $S$ on the beach. The point $S$ is $10\\,\\text{m}$ away from the lifeguard. If the balloon is rising at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the lifeguard and the balloon is increasing at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524031 1072 #uuid "edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524031 1074 "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1068 17592186523794 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1068 17592186523822 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1068 17592186523879 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1068 17592186523911 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1069 "q-334268" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1071 "![](images/97/97291bd2f6b0a958a6651a87076a8ea313bec31cd6004840e0d81247dc364bf8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i},$ where $a,b\\in R,$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1072 #uuid "ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524032 1074 "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{clockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524033 1068 17592186523904 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524033 1069 "q-143658" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524033 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524033 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}, & -1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524033 1072 #uuid "f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524033 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/19/19510c5eb927edbc93ff077f13e645820548e9074da0e1101d9ef641650642bb.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524034 1068 17592186523883 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524034 1069 "q-143320" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524034 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524034 1071 "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=75$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524034 1072 #uuid "f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524034 1074 "Since $n=75$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=75\\cdot 0.4=30>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=75\\cdot (1-0.4)=45>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{75}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.146\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524035 1068 17592186523848 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524035 1069 "q-128441" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524035 1070 17592186045487 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524035 1071 "What is $0÷(-\\frac{9}{7}).$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524035 1072 #uuid "f162fba1-f09f-5af9-a84b-e3875292cc2e" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524035 1074 "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n0÷(-\\frac{9}{7})=0.\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524036 1068 17592186523796 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524036 1069 "q-122971" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524036 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524036 1071 "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[-5,5].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524036 1072 #uuid "f1ddbd77-4be4-59b3-aa0d-217f8714ae20" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524036 1074 "Note that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[-5,5],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}-{y}_{0})+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})+({y}_{3}-{y}_{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}-{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}}-{y}_{0})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{y}_{3}}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}+{y}_{n} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-{y}_{0} \\\\  & =5-(-5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524037 1068 17592186523918 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524037 1069 "q-121675" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524037 1070 17592186045486 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524037 1071 "![](images/56/561e9e9bd41dddff575a95ed619d2ff3b91a48e0845960a6265c7c7cb1dc04fe.png)\n\nA  $50\\,\\text{cm}$ beverage dispenser is being emptied at a constant rate of  $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the dispenser is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ in radius. How fast is the beverage level dropping when there is $576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of beverage remaining in the funnel?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524037 1072 #uuid "f35215fb-23bc-5523-aa38-ea3ed45464a8" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524037 1074 "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=50\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=50\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{50} \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(h)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =1\\,728 \\\\ h & =12\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\pi  & =\\pi (12)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -16 & =144\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the beverage level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524038 1068 17592186523852 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524038 1069 "q-121345" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524038 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524038 1071 "![](images/96/96b70c61e20b693a29a125b5f03243a75d3bfd0aeb051a2f6f74525cfdb3da78.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and flies at a constant angle of ${45}^{∘}$ to the horizontal. How fast is the drone gaining altitude if its speed is $42\\,\\text{km/h}?$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524038 1072 #uuid "f624216d-33b0-5bec-b567-6dca0950cf8b" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524038 1074 "![](images/40/40e0ae889d241c45464492ba3842d586effe528ab93b3554f6dbfb01d215c308.png)\n\nLet $y$ be the drone's altitude and $z$ be the actual distance between the drone and its take-off point, as shown below.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=42\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 42=21\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524039 1068 17592186523871 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524039 1069 "q-97686" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524039 1070 17592186045485 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524039 1071 "Lucy's marbles are labeled with numbers $-3$ through $8.$ If Lucy chooses a marble at random, what is the expected value of the chosen marble?" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524039 1072 #uuid "f8cca8da-6044-5230-8f59-af0159f2bec9" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524039 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{-3,-2,…,8\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{-3,-2,…,8\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-3+8}{2}=2.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen marble is $2.5.$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524040 1062 #uuid "01296d4e-52f1-5b16-9285-4d32f1136eff" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524040 1063 17592186523937 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524041 1058 17592186524107 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186524041 1062 #uuid "03875695-6306-58ee-ade0-e90c339315ac" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true]